   "applicability": "The Region B instruments are relevant because the recipient is established in Region B and the proposed transfer will be stored and accessed there. However, the Health Records Act's operative duties are party-specific. The transferred material is a health record under Section 2 because it identifies an individual and relates to physical health and the provision of health care. Harrow is not a covered provider: it does not bill a Region B-licensed health insurer electronically and is not an insurer. It is not a service agent because it keeps the information in its own clinical record for its own care, rather than on behalf of a covered provider. It is also not a data host because it does not store records on behalf of another person. The Records Access Act is relevant only as a potential power applicable to a Region B computing provider; no facts show that Harrow provides storage, hosting or computing services as a business.",
   "coverage_note": "I checked the Health Records Act definitions and application provisions, then Sections 4, 6, 7 and 9 identified in the supervisor focus, including their alternative routes and exceptions. I also checked the Records Access Act definitions and Sections 3 through 6 concerning production, non-disclosure, encryption and review. The supplied facts do not make Harrow a covered provider, service agent, data host or computing provider, so the principal Region B duties are not engaged. The absence of Trusted Recipient Scheme certification has no consequence in the provisions reviewed: none makes certification a condition of receiving or retaining records. This assessment does not determine duties imposed by Region A or Subregion A1 law on the sender, including any rule governing an A1 practitioner's disclosure or cross-border transfer."
